timestamp=2026-11-04T19:17:43.415Z level=INFO run=c2c30b4a message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\n# Group by billing periods that start on weekday 1 (Tue)\n# For each date, find which billing period it belongs to: period_start = date - days_since_last_period_start\n# or easier: (date - earliest).days; then divide by 7? Or find the Tuesday that starts the billing cycle\n# Alternatively, for each date, the billing month is determined by which billing period bucket it falls into\n# and billing periods start every 7 days from earliest? Or maybe each \"month\" means calendar month - but need to reconcile\n# But let us look at how many entries fall into each Tuesday-started week\ndates = []\nimport csv\nwith open('/app/dataset/timesheet.csv') as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        try:\n            d = datetime.strptime(row['date'], '%Y-%m-%d')\n            dates.append(d)\n        except:\n            pass\n\nearliest = min(dates)\n# Find start of billing week for each date: start is the most recent Tue <= d? Or d is in week starting Tue\n# Week starts on Tue, so days since start of that billing week = (d.weekday() - start_day) mod 7\nstart_day = earliest.weekday()\nby_week = {}\nfor d in dates:\n    delta = (d.weekday() - start_day) % 7\n    week_start = d - timedelta(days=delta)\n    key = week_start.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')\n    by_week.setdefault(key, []).append(d)\n\n# sort by week\nfor k, v in sorted(by_week.items())[:5]:\n    print(k, len(v), v[0].strftime('%a'), '-', v[-1].strftime('%a'))\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
