timestamp=2026-11-04T19:25:18.269Z level=INFO run=c2c30b4a message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\n# Group by billing period starting on Tue (weekday 1). Each billing period is one \"billing month\"?\n# But usually billing periods are cycles - maybe 4 weeks per period? Or just aligned to calendar month boundaries in some way?\n# The phrase is a bit confusing: \"For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record.\"\n# So in each calendar month, the billing period starts on that weekday. That means the billing period for that month may start mid-month or on that specific Tue of that month?\n# Or perhaps the \"billing month\" is the month whose name is used, and the billing cycle for that month starts on the Tue (of week) that is the first occurrence of that weekday in the month, or the one inherited from earliest record applied to that month.\n# Also earliest is 2025-03-04, which is a Tuesday. 2025-03-04 is in March - maybe the billing period for March starts on 2025-03-04 (the Tue). And subsequent billing periods start on the same weekday each month? Or every 4 weeks? Or same weekday of each month (first Tue of each month)?\n# Let us look at the pattern. Also the answer needs to have \"salaries should be sorted by billing month in ascending order\". The values are just integers - maybe 4 values per employee (Mar, Apr, May, Jun) if 4 billing months total?\n# Let us count total rows grouped by calendar month: 4 calendar months. That fits - could be monthly by calendar month. Also the \"billing period starts on same day of week...\" might just mean that payment/salary is processed on that day of the week (operational detail), not affecting grouping.\n# That is plausible. Let us try grouping by calendar month (year, month).\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
