timestamp=2026-11-04T19:27:20.533Z level=INFO run=c2c30b4a message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# If we group by billing period starting on Tue, we get 17 periods total - that means 17 \"billing months\" (cycles), so each employee would have 17 salary values.\n# That seems much more granular than calendar months (4). Which one is correct?\n# The task says \"monthly salary for each employee for every month\" - calendar month is the natural interpretation of \"month\".\n# Also the note about billing period starting on the same day of week inherited from earliest record could be describing when salaries are paid or how the billing cycle is structured. But for aggregation into \"monthly\" totals, typically you group by calendar month.\n# Let us also look at the total dataset size and the fact that dates span Mar-Jun (4 calendar months). 17 periods of ~1 week each is 17*7 ~ 119 days; from Mar 4 to end of Jun is roughly ~118 days, so that matches weekly billing cycles. That fits the phrase about billing period starting on that weekday.\n# Also \"monthly salary\" in that context could mean salary per billing period (billing month) where billing is weekly-based? Or the term \"month\" is used to mean billing period? That is possible. Let us look at the expected answer format more: \"list of integer salaries. For each PESEL, the list of salaries should be sorted by billing month in ascending order.\"\n# Also the example in the answer template shows 4 values - if 17 periods, we'd get 17. With 4 calendar months, 4. That suggests 4 billing months corresponding to calendar months? Or maybe the \"billing month\" is named/identified by calendar month in practice?\n# Another interpretation: the billing month is the calendar month in which the billing period's start falls. Since periods start on Tue, and the first billing period starts on 2025-03-04 (Tue in March), so billing month is March. Next billing period starts 2025-03-11 (Tue) - still March? 2025-03-11 is March. 2025-03-18 March, 2025-03-25 March. 2025-04-01 is Tue in April - so that becomes April billing month. That matches! So billing periods (cycles starting on Tue) are grouped by the calendar month of their period start. That gives us 4 billing months: Mar, Apr, May, Jun.\n# That fits perfectly. So \"billing month\" for each billing period is the month of the period's start date.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
